  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Prove that there are no integers $a$ and $b$ for which $a^2-4b=3$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "For even $a=2k$, the equation gives $4(k^2-b)=3$. For odd $a=2k+1$, it gives $4(k^2+k-b)=2$. Neither is possible because neither 3 nor 2 is divisible by 4.",
      "worked": "Suppose such integers $a$ and $b$ exist. Then $a^2=4b+3$.\n\nIf $a$ is even, write $a=2k$ for an integer $k$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}(2k)^2&=4b+3\\\\4k^2&=4b+3\\\\4(k^2-b)&=3.\\end{align*}$$\nThis is impossible because the left-hand side is divisible by 4 and the right-hand side is not.\n\nIf $a$ is odd, write $a=2k+1$ for an integer $k$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}(2k+1)^2&=4b+3\\\\4k^2+4k+1&=4b+3\\\\4(k^2+k-b)&=2.\\end{align*}$$\nAgain, the left-hand side is divisible by 4 and the right-hand side is not. Both cases lead to a contradiction. Therefore, no such integers exist.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 108
  },
